Aim
To demonstrate direct-sequence CDMA by spreading the data of three users with orthogonal Walsh codes and recovering each user's data by despreading.
Detailed Algorithm
1.  Start. Define the 4 x 4 Walsh-Hadamard matrix; each row is the spreading code of one user.
2.  Take 4 data bits per user and map them to BPSK (bit 1 becomes +1 and bit 0 becomes -1).
3.  Spreading: repeat each data symbol 4 times (chips) and multiply by the user's code repeated for every bit.
4.  Add the spread signals of all users to form the composite transmitted signal.
5.  Despreading: multiply the composite signal by the desired user's code, sum the 4 chips of every bit and divide by 4.
6.  Decide: positive correlation gives bit 1, otherwise bit 0.
7.  Compare the recovered bits with the transmitted bits. Stop.
